  "display_filter_note": "Danny (2026-09-28): SM-11-01 is now filtered exactly like SM-11-02 / 07 / 08 - his marked-up frame shows the four displays he wants watched are all mounted on the walls (one big 4K TV on the left wall plus three on the back wall); the bright desk monitors in the lower part of the frame are not displays of interest. The wall count is 3-4 detected panels depending on the frame, and picture sync is simply IN SYNC when everything that IS detected reports the same state. Danny (2026-09-23): for SM-11-02 / 07 / 08 only the two large LED TVs mounted on the wall count for the Idle and picture-sync verdicts. The small computer monitors sitting on the desks must be ignored. A wall display sits in the upper part of the camera frame, so any bright region whose vertical centre lies below max_center_y_frac of the frame height is treated as desk level and is dropped from the candidate list; it is still listed in the report under 'Ignored bright regions' with the reason. In these rooms OCR-only text with no detected region (a phantom) is also ignored, because it cannot be proven to belong to a wall TV. Rooms without an entry here keep the old behaviour (largest N regions, phantom takes a slot).",
  "displays_note": "Danny's confirmed inventory: SM-11-01 = 4 LED TV, SM-11-02 = 2 LED TV, SM-11-03..06 = 1 LED wall each, SM-11-07 = 2 LED TV, SM-11-08 = 2 LED TV, Common Area = 1 LED TV. Normally only the N largest bright regions are monitored (N = count) and smaller bright regions are traditional whiteboards / noticeboards that are ignored. EXCEPTION (Danny): any bright region whose OCR reads an AVoIP decoder standby banner IS one of the room's TVs, so it is always monitored even if it is not among the N largest.",
  "display_regions_note": "Danny (2026-09-29) RE-MARKED SM-11-01: the four regions below come from clipboard-2026-09-29T01-44-20-994Z-51166354.jpg (1733x967) and replaced the original four, which were offset - the old 'centre-left' box sat at x=703..845, which is where the CENTRE-RIGHT panel actually is, and that mis-placement is why the room reported '3 idle / 1 not idle -> CHECK REGION' on runs where every screen showed the same standby picture. Note the centre-left panel is TILTED in the room, so its axis-aligned bounding box is 156x184 (aspect 0.848) and that is correct; likewise the left-wall 4K reads aspect 1.34 because it hangs on an angled left wall. Danny (2026-09-28) marked the displays he wants watched by drawing a red box on the camera image. Those marks are the authoritative monitoring areas for this room: the engine measures the box directly and SKIPS bright-blob detection, because a display can legitimately be darker than the wall around it and no bright-blob rule can find it. SM-11-02 is the proof - both wall TVs show the Windows lock screen, so the panel mean is 98/99 against a wall of 129/171, and the best sliding-window candidate scored IoU 0.00 against Danny's boxes. SM-11-01 carries four wall TVs (one 4K on the left wall, three on the back/right wall) which the report shows as four separate monitored panels. Any bright region outside these boxes is still listed in the report for information, but is never counted towards the Idle / picture-sync verdict. Boxes are in 1280x720 camera-frame pixels; a 'frame' key rescales if the mark was measured at another size. Re-measure only when the camera is physically re-aimed. When a mark's own aspect ratio and the width-scaled mapping agree to within ~0.02, the mapping is already correct - do NOT edge-home on luminance alone, because a saturated-colour panel (Room 4's blue Windows wallpaper) will make a brightness scan lock onto dark CONTENT instead of the panel edge and make the box worse. Read the colour channel (B-R) to separate a dim panel edge from a bezel/shadow, and read it to find the true bottom edge - a glowing bottom bezel looks like panel to a luminance scan.",
